    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L14-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Even More Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Use the information your partner gave you about their polynomial to:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L14-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Even More Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Once you and your partner have finished graphing, check your factored form and graph with your partner and discuss any differences.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L14-ACTIVITY-029",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Even More Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We can look at the same polynomial in many different ways. Let’s think about \\(P(x) = x^3 - 7x + 6\\). It’s written in standard form, but we could also write it in factored form as \\((x-2)(x+3)(x-1)\\). If we graph \\(P(x)\\), we get this:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L14-ACTIVITY-030",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Even More Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Depending on what we know about \\(P(x)\\) and what we want to do, different forms of it will be more useful. If we want to quickly estimate the value of \\(P(x)\\) for some value of \\(x\\), the graph might be most helpful. If we don’t know…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L14-ACTIVITY-031",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Even More Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "How the outputs of a function change as we look at input values further and further from 0.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L14-ACTIVITY-032",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Even More Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "This function shows different end behavior in the positive and negative directions. In the positive direction the values get larger and larger. In the negative direction the values get closer and closer to -3.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L14-ACTIVITY-033",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Even More Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The power to which a factor occurs in the factored form of a polynomial. For example, in the polynomial \\((x-1)^2(x+3)\\), the factor \\(x-1\\) has multiplicity 2 and the factor \\(x+3\\) has multiplicity 1.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L14-PRACTICE-001",
      "SourceSection": "Problem 1",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We know these things about a polynomial function, \\(f(x)\\): it has exactly one relative maximum and one relative minimum, it has exactly three zeros, and it has a known factor of \\((x-4)\\). Sketch a graph of \\(f(x)\\) given this…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L14-PRACTICE-002",
      "SourceSection": "Problem 2",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Mai graphs a polynomial function, \\(f(x)\\), that has three linear factors \\((x+6)\\), \\((x+2)\\), and \\((x-1)\\). But she makes a mistake. What is her mistake?",
